{"id": 3781, "date": "2016-11-13T01:00:20", "date_gmt": "2016-11-13T06:00:20", "guid": {"rendered": "http://ravencypresswood.com/?p=3781"}, "modified": "2016-11-13T09:06:19", "modified_gmt": "2016-11-13T14:06:19", "slug": "the-field-of-accumulation-the-ocean-of-khandro", "status": "publish", "type": "post", "link": "https://ravencypresswood.com/2016/11/13/the-field-of-accumulation-the-ocean-of-khandro/", "title": {"rendered": "The Field of Accumulation: The Ocean of Khandro"}, "content": {"rendered": "<div id=\"attachment_2536\" style=\"width: 605px\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\"><a href=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/tsok-zhing-according-to-menri.jpg?ssl=1\"><img data-recalc-dims=\"1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-2536\" data-attachment-id=\"2536\" data-permalink=\"https://ravencypresswood.com/2014/12/06/the-field-of-accumulation-abbots-of-menri-monastery/tsok-zhing-according-to-menri/\" data-orig-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/tsok-zhing-according-to-menri.jpg?fit=1580%2C1976&amp;ssl=1\" data-orig-size=\"1580,1976\" data-comments-opened=\"0\" data-image-meta=\"{&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;1&quot;}\" data-image-title=\"Tsok zhing according to Menri\" data-image-description=\"\" data-image-caption=\"\" data-large-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/tsok-zhing-according-to-menri.jpg?fit=819%2C1024&amp;ssl=1\" class=\" wp-image-2536\" src=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/tsok-zhing-according-to-menri.jpg?resize=595%2C744&#038;ssl=1\" alt=\"Tsok zhing according to Menri\" width=\"595\" height=\"744\" /></a><p id=\"caption-attachment-2536\" class=\"wp-caption-text\">Tsok zhing according to MenriThe Yungdrung Bon Merit Field of the Menri Tradition</p></div>\n<p>In the Yungdrung Bön tradition, the place where the deities and objects of veneration are gathered is called the <span style=\"color:#000080;\"><strong><em>&#8220;Tsok Zhing&#8221;, </em></strong></span>the <span style=\"color:#000080;\"><strong>&#8220;Field of Accumulation&#8221;. </strong> </span>It is also sometimes translated as the <strong><span style=\"color:#000080;\">&#8220;Field of Merit&#8221;</span></strong> or the <span style=\"color:#000080;\"><strong>&#8220;Merit Field&#8221;</strong></span> because what is being accumulated by paying homage and making offerings to this place is merit, or virtue.  This is the Merit Field according to the Menri Tradition from a drawing done by the great master and scholar HE Yongdzin Tenzin Namdak Rinpoche.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/10/outline-guide-for-tsok-zhing-ocean-of-khandros.jpg?ssl=1\"><img data-recalc-dims=\"1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-attachment-id=\"3787\" data-permalink=\"https://ravencypresswood.com/2016/11/13/the-field-of-accumulation-the-ocean-of-khandro/outline-guide-for-tsok-zhing-ocean-of-khandros/\" data-orig-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/10/outline-guide-for-tsok-zhing-ocean-of-khandros.jpg?fit=1166%2C1556&amp;ssl=1\" data-orig-size=\"1166,1556\" data-comments-opened=\"1\" data-image-meta=\"{&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;1&quot;}\" data-image-title=\"outline-guide-for-tsok-zhing-ocean-of-khandros\" data-image-description=\"\" data-image-caption=\"\" data-large-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/10/outline-guide-for-tsok-zhing-ocean-of-khandros.jpg?fit=767%2C1024&amp;ssl=1\" class=\"aligncenter wp-image-3787\" src=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/10/outline-guide-for-tsok-zhing-ocean-of-khandros.jpg?resize=587%2C784&#038;ssl=1\" alt=\"outline-guide-for-tsok-zhing-ocean-of-khandros\" width=\"587\" height=\"784\" /></a></p>\n<p><strong><span style=\"color:#000080;\">The</span></strong> <span style=\"color:#000080;\"><strong>Ocean of Khandro</strong></span> are depicted as figures #1-25.   Khandro (Sanskrit: dakini) literally translates as &#8216;sky-goer&#8217; or &#8216;one who moves in the sky&#8217;.  Khandro are a class of female beings who protect and serve the religious tradition as well as bestow spiritual attainments.   Khandro can be classified in many ways.  The classification below is according to the <em><strong>Tsok Zhing</strong></em> text.  Each of the khandro are adorned with the six bone ornaments.</p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\"><strong>#1-8 Wisdom Khandro</strong></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">1.<span style=\"color:#000080;\"> </span>Kalpa Zangmo, </span> she has one face and four arms<br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">2. Miyo Tenma</span><br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">3. Dekma Kündrol</span><br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">4. Kyema Ötso, </span>Khandro of the Mother Tantra<br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">5. Namkha Déden</span><br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">6. Dakpa Kündü</span><br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">7. Salwa Dröbéb</span><br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">8. Tukjé Kündrol, </span>Khandro of Tummo and Tsa Lung</p>\n<div id=\"attachment_3798\" style=\"width: 519px\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\"><a href=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/11/kalpa-zangmo.jpg?ssl=1\"><img data-recalc-dims=\"1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-3798\" data-attachment-id=\"3798\" data-permalink=\"https://ravencypresswood.com/2016/11/13/the-field-of-accumulation-the-ocean-of-khandro/kalpa-zangmo/\" data-orig-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/11/kalpa-zangmo.jpg?fit=1500%2C2000&amp;ssl=1\" data-orig-size=\"1500,2000\" data-comments-opened=\"1\" data-image-meta=\"{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;2.7&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;Canon PowerShot ELPH 300HS&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;1478938951&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;4.3&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;640&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0.033333333333333&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;1&quot;}\" data-image-title=\"kalpa-zangmo\" data-image-description=\"\" data-image-caption=\"\" data-large-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/11/kalpa-zangmo.jpg?fit=768%2C1024&amp;ssl=1\" class=\"wp-image-3798\" src=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/11/kalpa-zangmo.jpg?resize=509%2C678&#038;ssl=1\" width=\"509\" height=\"678\" /></a><p id=\"caption-attachment-3798\" class=\"wp-caption-text\">Khandro Kalpo Zangmo</p></div>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\"><strong>#9-13 Action Khandro</strong></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">9a. Ürgyen Khandro, Khandro from Ürgyen</span><br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">9b. Ürgyen Khandro, Khandro from Ürgyen</span><br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">9c. Ürgyen Khandro, Khandro from Ürgyen</span><br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">9d. Ürgyen Khandro, Khandro from Ürgyen</span><br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">10. Choza Bönmo, </span>Khandro of the female lineage of the Yétri Tasel Dzogchen<br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">11. Öden Barma, </span>Consort of Yidam Drenpa Namkha<br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">12a. Gyagar Khandro, Khandro from India</span><br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">12b. Gyagar Khandro, Khandro from India</span><br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">12c. Gyagar Khandro, Khandro from India</span><br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">12d. Gyagar Khandro, Khandro from India</span><br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">13. Drushé Khandro, </span><span style=\"color:#000080;\">Khandro from Drusha</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\"><strong>#14-25 Animal-headed Khandro</strong></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">14.</span> <span style=\"color:#000080;\">Durjé Ü</span>, Heron-headed Khandro<br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">15.</span> <span style=\"color:#000080;\">Pharwé Ü</span>, Coyote-headed Khandro<br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">16.</span> <span style=\"color:#000080;\">Chusin Ü</span>, Crocodile-headed Khandro<br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">17.</span> <span style=\"color:#000080;\">Duktrul Ü</span>, Poisonous Snake-headed Khandro<br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">18.</span> <span style=\"color:#000080;\">Chè Chang Ü</span>, Jackal-headed Khandro<br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">19.</span> <span style=\"color:#000080;\">Sengé Ü</span>, Lion-headed Khandro<br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">20.</span> <span style=\"color:#000080;\">Khyungi Ü</span>, Garuda-headed Khandro<br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">21.</span> <span style=\"color:#000080;\">Takgi Ü</span>, Tiger-headed Khandro<br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">22</span>. <span style=\"color:#000080;\">Zikgi Ü</span>, Leopard-headed Khandro<br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">23.</span> <span style=\"color:#000080;\">Domgi Ü</span>, Bear-headed Khandro<br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">24.</span> <span style=\"color:#000080;\">Drékyi Ü</span>, Yellow Bear-headed Khandro<br />\n<span style=\"color:#000080;\">25.</span> <span style=\"color:#000080;\">Khaté Ü</span>, Crow-headed Khandro</p>\n<p>Raven Cypress Wood ©2015</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n", "protected": false}, "excerpt": {"rendered": "<p>In the Yungdrung Bön tradition, the place where the deities and objects of veneration are gathered is called the &#8220;Tsok Zhing&#8221;, the &#8220;Field of Accumulation&#8221;.  It is also sometimes translated as the &#8220;Field of Merit&#8221; or the &#8220;Merit Field&#8221; because what is being accumulated by paying homage and making offerings to this place is merit, or virtue.  [&hellip;]</p>\n", "protected": false}, "author": 44992789, "featured_media": 0, "comment_status": "open", "ping_status": "open", "sticky": false, "template": "", "format": "image", "meta": {"_coblocks_attr": "", "_coblocks_dimensions": "",